# Refined Question & Improved Triggers

**Improved question:** Which activities must be sequenced because one produces an input for the next (mixing before resting, resting before cooking)? Which activities can run in parallel because they use different resources or stations? Which activities must be synchronized or held back because they're tied to a deadline (coffee must finish brewing when pancakes finish cooking; everything must be ready to serve hot at 10:00)? What must preheat or rest before use (oven, griddle, batter)? What must be held or kept warm after finishing (bacon, pancakes, fruit chilled)?

**Improved answer & revised triggers:**

- **Bacon track:** Preheat oven → lay bacon → bake bacon → drain bacon. The bacon must stay warm until serving.
- **Pancake track:** Mix dry → beat wet → combine → rest batter (while bacon bakes) → preheat griddle → cook pancakes. Griddle heats on a burner while batter rests.
- **Fruit track:** Prep fruit → chill fruit (must be chilled for 15 min before serving; chill can run in parallel with other work).
- **Coffee track:** Grind & setup → brew coffee (timed to finish exactly when pancakes finish, so they come hot to the table).
- **Serve track:** Waits for bacon hot, pancakes hot, fruit chilled, coffee ready, then brings everything out.

The critical insight: **coffee must finish brewing at the same moment pancakes finish cooking.** To achieve this, coffee setup and brewing are delayed so they end at the same time as the griddle work. The fruit salad must be chilled 15+ minutes before serving. Everything converges on the serve step.

